<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- The manifest of an executable built on wxl, an application or a tool of the
     library's own, written once for all of them and generated for each: the
     only thing that differs between them is the name in the identity, and
     wxl_target_manifest() knows it already.

     What it says is required by the library, not by the executable. An
     unpackaged WinUI3 process has to name the Windows it was tested against,
     or the framework refuses to consider it a current-Windows one, and it has
     to declare that it handles per-monitor DPI itself, or its window is
     bitmap-stretched on a scaled display. Neither can be said from code: a
     linker directive adds dependencies only, which is how the other half of
     this (version 6 of the common controls) already travels, from a
     #pragma comment(linker, ...) in wxl.ui/src/launch.cpp.

     The active code page (Windows 10 1903 and later) makes the process's ANSI
     and OEM code pages UTF-8: GetACP() and GetOEMCP() answer 65001 instead of
     the system's own, 1251 and 866 on a Russian Windows. With it, measured
     against the same program without it: path::string() gives UTF-8 where it
     threw on a character the system's page lacks; a path made from a
     std::string of UTF-8 round-trips where it was read as that page; a
     std::ifstream opens a file named by such a std::string, which it did not
     open before; std::getenv answers in UTF-8 instead of that page. So code in
     the process that is not wxl's and goes through these keeps names intact.
     It is not what makes wxl's own paths right, which stay UTF-16 up to the
     system call: an unpaired surrogate in a name still throws from
     path::string(), and std::getenv still answers from the copy of the
     environment the C runtime took at startup.

     An executable with reasons of its own passes a manifest in its sources,
     and then this one steps aside: two manifests with different identities do
     not merge, and the link fails outright ("Values of attribute name not
     equal in different manifest snippets"). -->
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="@WXL_APP_NAME@"/>
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <activeCodePage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">UTF-8</activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <maxversiontested Id="10.0.22621.0"/>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
